 "limits": [
  "immunofluorescence with one antibody per gene in a small panel of cell lines (mostly U-2 OS, A-431, U-251MG) -- NOT K562, so a location here is not a measurement of the cell being modelled and compartment can be cell-type dependent",
  "antibody-based: an unvalidated antibody gives a wrong location, which is why Reliability is carried per gene and 792 Uncertain calls are kept but flagged rather than deleted",
  "31% of cell genes have no HPA annotation, and the hole is not random (annotated genes have higher dep_frac and higher PPI degree, both p<1e-10) -- absence is stored as UNKNOWN and run as its own arm, never as `not co-localized`",
  "co-localization here is annotation overlap, not contact: two proteins in the nucleoplasm may never touch, and the shared-compartment test cannot distinguish that from a real interface",
  "the 12-word `comp` vocabulary contains two words (membrane, extracellular) that HPA's IF vocabulary cannot express at all, so the agreement figure is computed on the remainder and says nothing about those genes",
  "HPA's `Vesicles` term pools endosomes, lysosomes, peroxisomes, lipid droplets and secretory vesicles; it is left unmapped rather than forced into one of the 12, so 2,488 genes are outside the agreement figure",
  "steady-state localization of an unperturbed cell: it says nothing about relocalization after a perturbation, which is what a dynamic model would need",
  "the gating test measures whether shared compartment enriches for transcriptionally coupled PPI edges; it does NOT establish that non-co-localized edges are false",
  "`co-localized` is dominated by two terms -- nucleoplasm and cytosol account for most shared-location events -- so the pooled gating number is close to a nuclear/cytosolic indicator, which the gene-responsiveness covariate already partly encodes; the hub/non-hub split is what separates the two and it was prespecified, not chosen after seeing the pooled result",
  "the per-compartment breakdown is EXPLORATORY, not prespecified: it was run after the non-hub arm came back positive. Seven compartments were tested against one pool with no multiplicity correction, so the individual ratios are hypothesis-generating and the three positive ones need an independent replication before they are used as a gate",
  "the readout is transcriptional response in K562 CRISPRi. An interaction can be real, compartment-consistent and produce no transcriptional consequence, so this test bounds the DECISION VALUE of compartment gating for this readout only -- it is not a test of whether the interactions are physically real"
 ],
 "verdict": "The ungated PPI layer reproduces its reference figure (1.177x against 1.208x on 213,269 testable pairs), so this is the same pipeline the other layers were scored with. GATING ON SHARED COMPARTMENT IS A NULL AS USUALLY STATED, AND WHAT SURVIVES IS NOT `COMPARTMENT` BUT 3 PARTICULAR COMPARTMENTS. Matched head to head on perturbation strength and gene responsiveness over 20 draws of 59,443 pairs each, co-localized PPI edges reach mean |z| 1.2425 against 1.2406 for non-co-localized (1.001x, median p 0.00762, 85% of draws p<0.05). Unmatched, the co-localized arm looks clearly better (1.344 vs 1.241 raw mean |z|, 1.183x vs 1.158x against their own non-linked controls); essentially all of that gap is perturbation strength and gene responsiveness, and it disappears when those are matched. The null has a specific cause: nucleoplasm and cytosol alone account for 71% of all shared-location events, so `co-localized` mostly means `both are nuclear or both are cytosolic`, which the gene-responsiveness covariate already encodes -- sharing ONLY a hub scores 0.979x (0% of draws p<0.05). Requiring the shared location to be outside those hubs does separate (1.5076 vs 1.4332, 1.052x, median p 1.28e-10, 100% of draws, n=26,678), and it survives the adversarial round: 1.047x with locations-per-pair matched exactly, 1.082x against hub-co-localized pairs rather than against nothing, 1.063x on Enhanced|Supported annotations only, 1.024x at 50x50 bins. BUT THE PER-COMPARTMENT BREAKDOWN IS WHERE THE HONEST ANSWER IS, and it is not uniform: the whole effect sits in Endoplasmic reticulum 1.131x, Nucleoli 1.117x, Mitochondria 1.033x, while Vesicles 0.923x, Nuclear speckles 0.951x, Plasma membrane 0.951x, Golgi apparatus 0.978x are at or below 1.0. Those winners are the compartments with dedicated, transcriptionally co-regulated biogenesis programs (ribosome biogenesis, mitochondrial import and OXPHOS, the secretory/UPR machinery); the losers are traffic destinations, where two proteins can share an address without sharing a regulatory program. So the usable rule is not `gate PPI on shared compartment` -- that buys 0.999x, nothing -- but `co-residence in Endoplasmic reticulum, Nucleoli or similar biogenesis compartments is worth 13% at best`, which is around co-dependency (1.070x) and well below PPI itself (1.208x). Separately from the gating result, the layer fixes a real data-structure error: HPA gives 61% of the 13,599 annotated genes MORE THAN ONE location (mean 2.04, 8,325 genes), which the one-word `comp` field structurally cannot hold, and where both vocabularies can speak `comp` is inside the HPA set for 75% of 9,411 genes against a 29% label-shuffled baseline. Absence of an HPA call is kept as UNKNOWN and run as its own arm (1.180x -- indistinguishable from the annotated arms, so the missingness does not bias the split), never folded into the negative class."
